The code above fails and throws a RangeError because d2 contains a calendar unit (months),
which has an uneven and variable length.

Without a specific starting point on the calendar, the JavaScript runtime cannot determine
exactly how many days are in "1 month" to perform the comparison.

For example, 1 month starting in February could be 28 or 29 days, while 1 month starting in
January is 31 days.

The Temporal.Duration object does not have an equals() method because durations can be represented
in multiple "unbalanced" ways that are mathematically equivalent but structurally different.

For example, a duration of 90 seconds is logically equal to 1 minute and 30 seconds,
but they are stored as distinct property sets within the Temporal.Duration object.

Unbalanced Units: By default, Temporal.Duration preserves the units exactly as they were defined.
Because "60 minutes" and "1 hour" are different internal representations, a standard equals() method would
be ambiguous (should it check for identical properties or identical time length?)

Variable Unit Lengths: Units like days, months, and years do not have a fixed length.
A "month" can be 28, 30, or 31 days depending on the calendar and reference date.

Need for Context: To accurately compare two durations containing calendar units
(years, months, weeks), you must provide a relativeTo option (a specific starting point).
Without this reference, the API cannot determine if two calendar-based durations are truly "equal".

